Water-energy-food (WEF) system operation becomes challenging when reservoir, river, electricity, and agricultural observations are not updated in time for current decision making. This study develops a heterogeneous graph neural network combined with proximal policy optimization (GNN-PPO) for the Upper Yellow River WEF system under observation delays. The Upper Yellow River WEF system is explicitly mapped into a node-level heterogeneous graph of 24 interconnected reservoir, river, irrigation, electricity, pumping, and crop entities, enabling their physical and cross subsystems dependencies to be directly represented in the proposed framework. We use monthly data from 2001 to 2021 under no delay, subsystem delay, and node delay conditions. Under no delay, GNN-PPO achieved an average WEF penalty 2.5\% lower than that of PPO, although the difference was not statistically significant, indicating comparable nominal performance. In contrast, under observation delays of one to five months, the average WEF penalty of GNN-PPO was approximately 6.2\% lower than that of PPO, with a statistically significant performance difference (p = 0.027). Moreover, GNN-PPO remained largely insensitive to a five-month water-subsystem delay, whereas PPO exhibited a clear increase in average WEF penalty. Node-level analysis further identified the Hetao diversion interface (W8) as the most delay-sensitive location. Overall, the results show that graph-based approache is particularly valuable when observations are delayed and that timely information at key cross-system interfaces such as W8 is critical for robust WEF system management.
